I. INTRODUCTION
Wind turbines encounter unsteady conditions in the form of flow separation, dynamic stall, flow reattachment, and unsteady attached flow during most of their lifespan, resulting in substantial nonlinearity and hysteresis in the aerodynamic forces. Flow unsteadiness is the consequence of a variety of causes, such as tower shadow effects, yaw misalignment and wind shear, atmospheric turbulence, and the cyclic nature of vertical-axis wind turbine (VAWT) operation.1,2 These result in unsteady variations in the effective angles of attack, leading to nonlinear, time-dependent aerodynamic forces and fatigue loads on turbine blades. It is common to quantify the flow unsteadiness for an oscillating airfoil by the reduced frequency, k 1⁄4 xc/(2V1), where x is the pitching frequency, c is the airfoil chord,
and V1 is the wind speed.3 The angles of attack can reach 90 for
horizontal-axis wind turbines (HAWTs) and up to 180 for VAWTs.4 Nevertheless, the literature lacks unsteady airfoil data and prediction models over the whole operating range of wind turbine blades.5
Experimental unsteady airfoil data and high-fidelity models for high angles of attack are needed to develop accurate unsteady load predictions that can extend the life of wind turbines and improve the economics of harvesting wind energy. Unsteady aerodynamic forces can be modeled using computational fluid dynamics (CFD), empirical modeling, and data-driven models. CFD relies on physics modeling and requires significant computational resources, which hinders its utilization in iterative design or load-certification. Furthermore, the computed unsteady aerodynamic forces are often in disagreement with experimental data during flow separation and reattachment.6,7 Empirical models are the common approach adopted in academia and industry to reproduce dynamic stall loads for wind turbine design and aeroelastic simulations, in which unsteady aerodynamic forces are modeled based on physical intuition and engineering assumptions. However, no such empirical model has been developed to predict unsteady aerodynamic loads on airfoils or wind turbine blades for the entire operating range of angles
