of attack from 0 to 180 . For instance, OpenFAST, a wind turbine analysis code developed by the U.S. National Renewable Energy Laboratory, deactivates the unsteady forces computed by the unsteady aerodynamic empirical models when the estimated effective angle of attack exceeds 45 .8 A modified version of the ONERA (Office National d’etudes et de Recherches Aerospatiales) dynamic stall model for several airfoils was recently developed by Mohamed and Wood,2 with the goal of modeling the load variations associated with the shedding of multiple dynamic stall vortices (DSVs) up to angles of attack of 30 only. DSVs are the main unsteady flow feature associated with dynamic stall. They form and detach from near the leading edge as the load changes, and their passage over the airfoil further influences the lift and drag. At elevated angles of attack, it is difficult to tune empirical models over wide operating conditions, as the flow response is highly nonlinear.9 It was the difficulty in tuning the model of Ref. 2 for the present data that motivated us to investigate machine learning alternatives. Data-driven models have been demonstrated to be highly effective in a broad range of applications, including regression and classification problems, in which it is difficult to find the underlying physics.10–14 Recent advances in data science and machine learning have opened up new avenues for addressing otherwise intractable
aerodynamics and fluid mechanics challenges. Applications in aerodynamics include airfoil shape design and optimization,15,16 flow estimation and reconstruction,17,18 turbulence modeling for the Reynolds averaged Navier–Stokes equations,19 aeroelasticity,20 and load estimation for smart airfoils.21,22 To overcome unsteady aerodynamic modeling challenges, many machine learning techniques have been developed, including support vector machine (SVM),23 neural network (NN),24,25 recurrent neural network (RNN) with gated recurrent unit (GRU),26 long short-term memory (LSTM),27,28 and adaptive network-based Fuzzy inference system (ANFIS).29 Machine learning algorithms may be trained using both force measurements in wind tunnels1,9 and from CFD simulations.24,26,30 All these models were trained to predict unsteady forces on airfoil models at angles of attack lower than 45 . Unsteady aerodynamic forces are time series data that exhibit an aerodynamic lag in the flow separation and reattachment on a pitched airfoil, causing hysteresis in the unsteady forces.2 An accurate deep learning algorithm must reproduce the time histories of the circulatory and noncirculatory components generated by variations in effective angles of attack caused by varying inflow conditions and blade structural response.31 The time dependencies in the unsteady forces can be learnt by a RNN
