information about the steady load curves, such as the static stall angle (ass) and load hysteresis that have strong relationships to the dynamic stall forces. Not representing these characteristics in the constructed features set requires the machine learning algorithm to differentiate between attached and separated flow regimes and to generate the unsteady force hysteresis. Extending the application of the machine learning framework to include fluid dynamics at high angles of attack exacerbates the problem’s complexity and makes it extremely nonlinear. This necessitates the extraction of novel discriminative features to capture the wide range of operating conditions and airfoil performance in a way that is not achievable with either conventional unsteady modeling approaches or previous data-driven models. The purpose of this work is to provide one of the first applications of deep learning algorithms, specifically the BiLSTM algorithm, for predicting the unsteady aerodynamic forces on an oscillating airfoil model for the entire operating range. The BiLSTM was chosen because, in comparison to the LSTM, it offers extra training by traveling both forward and backward through time-history data.37 The proposed BiLSTM algorithm is trained using a subset of experimental data measured in a wind tunnel for a NACA 0021 airfoil model and then validated against the remaining data set. We believe that the present experiments provide a significant challenge for machine learning given the complex aerodynamic processes involved with the large
