II. EXPERIMENT OVERVIEW
The wind tunnel experiment was carried out in a 1 1 m2 wind tunnel at the University of Calgary. A NACA 0021 airfoil model was used, with a chord length of c 1⁄4 0.185 m, an aspect ratio of 4.5 and a blockage ratio of 18.5% at a 1⁄4 90 . The airfoil model consisted of five 3D-printed sections glued together on a 16 mm steel shaft. The final assembly was sanded and coated with epoxy for a smooth surface finish. The experiment was conducted at V1 1⁄4 10 m/s, corresponding to Reynolds number, Re 1⁄4 120 000, with a turbulence intensity level of
0.3%. A timing-belt system, driven by a stepper motor, was used to pitch the airfoil model about the quarter-chord location, as illustrated in Fig. 1. The stepper motor was equipped with an optical encoder to measure a with a resolution of 0.0625 . The model was mounted vertically at the center of the wind tunnel test section, between two ATI (Industrial Automation) multi-axis force sensors, as illustrated in Fig. 1. The capacity of the lower force sensor was 32 N in the x- and ydirections, with a force resolution of 0.006 25 N, while the upper force transducer had a capacity of 20 N in the x- and y-directions, with a resolution of 0.005 N. The upper force transducer was mounted on a spherical rod end to rotate freely with the airfoil model. A National Instruments data acquisition card with a 16-bit resolution, NI-DAQ 6212, was connected to each force sensor. The two cards were synchronized to commence acquisition at the same time using an external trigger. Two horizontals 0.9 0.9 m2 acrylic end plates were used to alleviate the free-end effects. The end plates were equipped with a 3Dprinted superelliptic leading edge to reduce the probability of flow separation at the leading edge.38 No vertical end plates were used. The force transducers measured the total loads acting on the airfoils, including the inertial, frictional, and aerodynamic forces. For every test run, the true aerodynamic responses were found by subtracting the force measurements when the wind tunnel was on and off. The normal force, Cn, and chord-axis force, Ca, were measured and
