IV. DYNAMIC STALL MODEL
A BiLSTM algorithm was developed to reproduce the variations in the unsteady forces. The BiLSTM is an advanced variant of the LSTM algorithm with two LSTMs, as shown in Fig. 5; the first is fed by the time-history data and the second by its reverse form, with the
aim of improved learning of the long-term dependencies. This should increase the prediction accuracy when the amplitude of the angle variation is large.49 The algorithm is implemented in MATLAB using the deep learning toolbox without any auxiliary coding apart from input data handling and results processing. The BiLSTM consists of three stacked layers, each with 100 neurons; and each layer is followed by a 0.5 dropout layer to prevent overfitting as much as possible. Overfitting occurs when the model is too closely aligned with the training data that it cannot respond to the validation cases. With an initial learning rate of 0.01, the Adam optimizer was used to optimize the weight parameters of the network based on the loss function of the mean squared error. The experimental data were split into training (30%), validation (10%), and testing data (60%), Table I. This relatively small amount of training data for a regression problem, was chosen to examine the model generalization and robustness over a wide range of operating conditions. The training data were chosen to cover as much as possible of the operating conditions, particularly for the reduced frequency, to cover various degrees of flow unsteadiness. The model was trained on a workstation with an Intel Core i7–10850 CPU @ 2.7 GHz, Intel UHD graphics, and 32 GB of RAM. The main computational cost for training the proposed BiLSTM was the offline computation, which is very low in comparison to typical CFD computational cost.
