lift well for all but the highest a, and similarly for the drag for all but the two highest a. For am 1⁄4 130 , both the modeled lift and drag follow the steady trajectory for decreasing a whereas the measurements follow the increasing a trend. It is possible that the model could not be improved until we have a better understanding of the differences between the steady forces for increasing and decreasing a. The model is more accurate at high a when aa increases, Figs. 17 and 18, suggesting that the errors in the model just referred to, are due to restriction of the cycle to a-range where the steady hysteresis is important. Generally, the model is accurate at the highest am, Figs. 19 and 20, except that the high frequency oscillations noted above have reappeared in the drag in all parts of Fig. 20. It is also noted that the unsteadiness in the response of the lift and drag increases with am which may complicate the modeling of the response. Given that the basic structure of the model was not altered during the training over a wide range of k and am, its general ability to match the measured lift and drag for the sinusoidally pitched NACA 0021 section has been demonstrated to be good. The lift has been modeled more accurately than the drag, where the high-frequency oscillations needed at low a are amplified at high a and reduce the model accuracy.
VI. CONCLUSION
The key contribution of the present research is to explore the potential of a deep learning framework to predict the nonlinear unsteady forces on oscillating airfoils over the entire range of operation for wind turbine applications. Training, validation, and testing data were obtained from wind tunnel measurements for an oscillating
NACA 0021 airfoil model at a Reynolds number of 120 000 with oscillation amplitude up to 60 and reduced frequency up to 0.075. By training the model using 30% of the unsteady force measurements, the deep learning BiLSTM algorithm is capable of reproducing variations in unsteady forces at angles of attack ranging from 60 to 190 . A novel set of features, inspired by empirical models, was constructed to represent the operating conditions and airfoil characteristics. Accurate predictions were achieved in all regimes except when reproducing the load overshoots generated by the shedding of multiple dynamic stall vortices. The model can be easily incorporated in wind turbine design codes; however, further research should be done to expand and corroborate these preliminary findings by reproducing the impact of the shedding of multiple dynamic stall vortices on the unsteady loads around the static stall angle and in the reverse flow regime. The future research should also include an investigation of the accuracy and generality of the model to reproduce unsteady loading for other airfoil shapes at high angles of attack.
